ressor … Highly … EHV-28CC-R290 … £113.20`.  \n   Root cause in chain: The generator (Gemini 3.1 Pro) or research‑synthesis (MiMo v2.5‑Pro) split the compressor into a motor sub‑part and a full assembly, but both inherited the same fabricated SKU. No post‑generation uniqueness check exists.  \n   Fix recommendation: Add a post‑BoM coherence gate (part of G3 review‑completeness) that flags duplicate part numbers with differing line totals and forces a merge or human resolution before the report is rendered.\n\n2. (HIGH) — Bill of Materials (global) / Appendix A  \n   Finding: 67 of 141 part lines contain fabricated part numbers that fail verification against real distributors, rendering the BoM un‑procurable.  \n   Evidence: Appendix A lists `EV-8K-R290`, `B5-24-R290`, `EHV-28CC-R290`, `iQ-EC-150W`, etc. as “stripped (was fabricated)”.  \n   Root cause in chain: The generator stage (Gemini 3.1 Pro) hallucinates SKU‑like strings; the part‑verification step (4.5) does not enforce a strict “no unverified part in final BoM” policy, allowing fabricated numbers to persist with warnings.  \n   Fix recommendation: Modify the part‑verification gate (4.5) to remove any unverified SKU entirely and replace it with a generic part designation (e.g., `### TBD ###`) and a full performance specification, blocking promotion to the BoM until a real SKU or accepted TBD is present.\n\n3. (HIGH) — Bill of Materials / Cost Stack (page 1, “£430 per kW thermal installed…” paragraph)  \n   Finding: The installed‑cost comparison uses £600–£900/kW derived from 30 kW light‑commercial monoblocs, not from 8 kW residential units, making the “28% below typical” claim meaningless.  \n   Evidence: “Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) … = £600-900/kW … 30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics.”  \n   Root cause in chain: Engine C (reference anchor) matched the design against the wrong power‑class bin in the corpus (likely due to an imprecise cosine‑similarity query that ignored power scale).  \n   Fix recommendation: Constrain the Engine C reference‑anchor lookup to products within ±25 % of the design thermal output (8 kW), and add a power‑class mismatch flag that fires if no close match exists.\n\n4. (HIGH) — Regulatory & Compliance section (page 33) / G1b gate  \n   Finding: The report applies EU directives (EU 517/2014, PED 2014/68/EU, EMC 2014/30/EU) as mandatory for a product brief that targets only the UK market; post‑Brexit UK regulations (e.g., UK SI 2016/1105, UK SI 2016/1091, Fluorinated Greenhouse Gases Regulations 2015) are not substituted or even mentioned.  \n   Evidence: Compliance table lists `EU` jurisdiction for F‑gas, PED, MD, EMC, etc.; the G1b warning notes “WARN · JURISDICTION_SCOPE … Brief targets multiple regions (UK, EU) without an explicit harmonisation plan.”  \n   Root cause in chain: The G1b compliance gate (`class-standards.ts`) treats “UK” as a subset of “EU” and does not have a separate register of UK statutory instruments, causing it to output EU directives as mandatory.  \n   Fix recommendation: Extend `class-standards.ts` with a UK‑specific standards map (UK SI equivalents of all currently listed EU directives). The gate should select UK SIs when the detected jurisdiction is solely “UK” and trigger UKCA‑route compliance, not CE.\n\n5. (HIGH) — Module 3 / Bill of Materials (REFRIGERANT ROUTING)  \n   Finding: The R290 refrigerant charge is costed as one full 5 kg industrial cylinder per unit, although the system requires only 650 g; this inflates per‑unit cost by ~7‑8×.  \n   Evidence: BoM line `R290 Refrigerant Charge … BOC … R290-IND-5KG … Qty 1 … Unit £17.24 … Line £17.24`; design text says “charged with exactly 650 g … supplied in BOC R290-IND-5KG industrial cylinders”.  \n   Root cause in chain: Engine B (price estimates) has no amortisation rule for consumable bulk items; it treats the cylinder as a single‑use part.  \n   Fix recommendation: Add an `AmortizedConsumable` logic to Engine B that reads a usage‑per‑unit field from the research synthesis (here 650 g) and divides the container price (5 kg) into per‑charge cost.\n\n6. (MEDIUM) — Module 4, Sub‑module “Compressor and drives” / BoM  \n   Finding: The design lists a 100 W defrost heater cable (trace heater for drip pan) but does not demonstrate that this is sufficient to prevent ice build‑up at the low‑end operating temperature of ‑25 °C with typical air humidity.  \n   Evidence: “Raychem defrost heater cable (part QTVR-100W … rated 100 W)” – no supporting calculation or power‑density check.  \n   Root cause in chain: The G0 physics ledger (deterministic) did not contain a drip‑pan freeze‑protection rule; the heater was specified without a thermal mass/climate validation.  \n   Fix recommendation: Insert a freeze‑protection check in the G0 ledger (`class-floors.ts`) that requires at least 200 W/m² for drip‑pan area at minimum ambient, and flag any under‑sizing as a design‑scale mismatch.\n\n7. (MEDIUM) — Module 2 (Airflow Management) / Bill of Materials  \n   Finding: The 500 mm fan cowl shroud is sourced as a custom‑fabricated part from Ziehl‑Abegg with a part number `Cowl-500-GR` that does not exist; the appendix labels it “manual sourcing required” and effectively TBD.  \n   Evidence: Appendix A: `fan cowl shroud … MANUAL SOURCING REQUIRED … standard off-the-shelf shrouds rarely meet specific 520mm/120mm dimensions with BS EN 378 compliance`.  \n   Root cause in chain: The generator hallucinated a SKU that looks like a Ziehl‑Abegg format; the part‑verification gate (4.5) downgraded it to “uncertain” but did not force a generic placeholder.  \n   Fix recommendation: In the part‑verification gate, when a part is flagged as “manual sourcing required”, automatically strip the SKU and replace it with a generic `TBD_FAN_SHROUD_520MM` descriptor and the required performance spec, preventing the BoM from appearing grounded.\n\n8. (MEDIUM) — Global / K10 shadow analysis (Appendix B)  \n   Finding: A required safety interlock wire (HP/LP switch to compressor contactor) is noted as missing in the K10 reference graph, and no explicit harness part exists to satisfy it.  \n   Evidence: “Missing required edges (1) · safety_protection ” [Digital-24V] energy_conversion_transduction — HP switch (31.5 bar R32 / 26 bar R290) and LP switch hard-wired to compressor contactor coil — trips”.  \n   Root cause in chain: The structural‑gate router (K10) runs in shadow mode only; it does not enforce generation of physical wiring.  \n   Fix recommendation: Promote K10 to enforcing mode for safety‑critical edges. When a required edge lacks a corresponding part‑in‑BoM, the structural gate should inject a standard “Control‑safety harness” line item with cross‑reference to the connected modules.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We are asked to review an AI-generated engineering report for a residential air-source heat pump, identify errors/inconsistencies, and recommend code fixes. The report is quite long. We need to be brutal and focus on specific categories. Let's go through systematically.\n\nCategories: physics/thermo, UK MCS/regulatory, BoM realism, internal coherence, brief-vs-design mismatch, procurement realism, compliance scope/jurisdiction errors.\n\nI'll read the report, looking for issues. The report is for an 8 kW R290 ASHP for UK market.\n\nKey issues I see:\n\n1. Brief-vs-design mismatch: The brief says \"target rated thermal output 8 kW\" and the design parameters show 8 kW, but is that plausible for the components chosen? The compressor has \"2.5 kW electrical input\" (module 4, compressor and drives: Highly EHV-28CC-R290, rated 2.5 kW electrical input). With SCOP 3.5, thermal output would be 2.5 * 3.5 = 8.75 kW, but the SCOP is seasonal coefficient, not instantaneous COP. However, at design conditions, the COP should be higher than SCOP, so 2.5 kW might be plausible. But is the compressor rated 2.5 kW electrical or thermal? In compressor specs, often \"rated input power\" is electrical. So 2.5 kW electrical input at what condition? For an 8 kW heat pump, typical electrical input at rated conditions might be around 2.0-2.5 kW with COP of 3.5-4.0. So that seems okay. But check the compressor model: Highly EHV-28CC-R290. 28CC might indicate displacement 28 cc. For R290, typical compressor displacement for 8 kW might be larger. Let's see if that's plausible. I can't verify exact model, but it's a common series. Might be okay. But note that later in appendix A, the compressor is \"stripped\" and recommended as ATH280SD-C9EU or ASH286MV-C8DU. Those might have different ratings. I'll not flag that as HIGH unless obvious. But there is a potential mismatch: The compressor is listed twice? In module 4 sub-module \"Compressor and drives\", they list \"Highly bldc compressor motor (part EHV-28CC-R290)\" and then also \"Highly Compressor (part EHV-28CC-R290)\" in the BoM? Actually looking at BoM page 5: in Compressor and Drives, they have \"Bldc Compressor Motor\" line with Highly EHV-28CC-R290, £17.08, and \"Highly Compressor\" line with the same part number, £113.20. This is duplicate? Possibly the motor and the compressor shell are separate? But it says \"Highly Compressor\" as a separate line item, and the Bldc Compressor Motor might be the motor itself. But then the same part number appears twice. That could be an internal coherence error.\n\n2. Internal coherence: Module 4 lists a \"bldc compressor motor\" as a component of the compressor sub-module, and also a \"Highly compressor\" as a component. In the BoM, both appear as separate line items with different unit costs (£17.08 vs £113.20) but the same part number (EHV-28CC-R290). This is contradictory. Also, in appendix A, both are flagged as stripped, with different recommended replacements (ATH280SD-C9EU and ASH286MV-C8DU). So the AI generated two separate parts erroneously.\n\n3. BoM realism: Many unit prices are implausibly low or high. For example, the refrigerant charge: BOC R290-IND-5KG with 650g charge, unit price ~£17.24 for 5 kg cylinder, but they only charge 650g, so cost per charge is just £17.24? Likely they'd buy bulk or per cylinder. £17.24 for a 5 kg cylinder seems low; a full 5 kg R290 cylinder might be ~£100+. And they priced at £17.24 per unit? They might be using the cylinder cost as the line item, but they'd need one cylinder per unit? No, one cylinder can fill many heat pumps. The BoM price should reflect the cost of 650g of refrigerant, not the whole cylinder. If the cylinder costs £100, 650g would be £13. So maybe £17.24 is plausible. But they have \"R290-IND-5KG\" as the part, quantity 1, unit £17.24, line £17.24. That seems to treat the cylinder as a direct material cost. That's okay if they amortize the cylinder. Not a major error. However, many prices are suspiciously uniform: many web estimates are £17.24, £7.81, £13.42, £4.64, £0.21, £0.01. These look like placeholder values, not real prices. The BoM says \"129 web estimates\", but the unit prices are mostly ~17.24, 7.81, 13.42, etc. These are likely generated by the engine's price estimator without real data. That's a realism issue. The report notes \"Scale factor: 0.325 — per-class batch-economics multiplier applied to distributor-quoted lines\". But the BoM is full of estimates. The cost stack shows raw BoM £1,762.21, with OEM price £2,234.27, which seems very low for an 8 kW ASHP. A real 8 kW monobloc typically costs OEM around £2,500-£3,500? But they claim £430/kW installed, 28% below typical range £600-£900. That's for a 30 kW unit mis-scaled? Actually the small domestic 8 kW unit might be £600-900/kW installed, so £430/kW would be unrealistic. They claim it's within engineering noise but then flag \"Multiplier 4.36× overshoots Grok market by >400% — both the raw BoM band ... and the COST_STACK install factor (I=0.80) appear too generous for the 30 kW class.\" That's incoherent: they're comparing with 30 kW light-commercial units? The report later in the BoM summary says \"Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) + scaled pipework + electrics + MCS commissioning = £18-27k total = £600-900/kW. 30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics.\" So they derived the £600-900/kW from 30 kW units, not 8 kW. That's a scale mismatch. The installed ASP of £3,441 for 8 kW corresponds to £430/kW, but the typical range for 8 kW might be similar £600-900, so the design is 28% below that. But the justification uses 30 kW data, which is smaller £/kW? Actually 30 kW units would have lower £/kW due to economies, so £600-900 for 30 kW means for 8 kW it would be even higher, so the design's £430/kW is even more unrealistic. So this is an internal coherence error (using wrong comparison scale) and a cost realism issue. But the main error: the installed cost seems too low.\n\n4. UK MCS regulatory gap: The compliance section lists BS EN 14825, BS EN 14511, etc., but the report says \"UK MCS\" is not yet added as mandatory in the class registry, but it's \"de-facto mandatory for residential UK market.\" However, they note a WARN: \"1 mandatory standard not declared in the brief for the detected jurisdictions (UK, EU).\" That is EMC 2014/30/EU. So that's a gap; they need EMC directive compliance. That's a HIGH regulatory gap. The report itself notes it, but it's still missing from the design. The product must comply. The BoM includes EMC filters and ferrite chokes, so maybe they're addressing it, but the directive is not formally included in the brief. This could be a HIGH regulatory gap, but it's flagged. Since the review is to find errors, we can note it.\n\n5. Physics/thermo: Any violations? The defrost heater is 100 W. Is that enough to defrost an 8 kW unit? Typically defrost energy may be a few kW for a short time, but 100 W seems low. However, a 100 W trace heater on the drip pan may be separate from a reverse-cycle defrost that uses hot gas. The report says the defrost cycle uses the 4-way reversing valve for active defrost (Module 3). So the drip pan heater is just to prevent ice buildup in the pan. 100 W could be plausible for that. But the Raychem QTVR-100W is a 100 W heating cable, 4 m length. That might be fine. No obvious physics error.\n\n6. Internal coherence: Multiple references to \"30 kW\" in the cost justification while the design is 8 kW. The report on page 1 of BoM: \"Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: ...\" then \"30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics. Multiplier 4.36× overshoots Grok market by >400% ... Flagged for COST_STACK calibration.\" This is nonsense mixing scales. So internal coherence error between 8 kW and 30 kW.\n\n7. Procurement realism: Many part numbers are fabricated. The report itself says 67 stripped and many uncertain. For a \"startup paying £20/month for this PDF\", the BoM is full of fabricated SKUs, which is a high procurement realism error. We should call out that the engine is generating fake part numbers. The fix is in the part-verification engine (Stage 4.5 maybe) and the supplier lookup (Engine D). The chain should not output a BoM with fabricated numbers; it should flag TBD or not generate them. This is a major failing.\n\n8. Brief-vs-design mismatch: The brief specifies a maximum gross mass of 90 kg. The report says that's the constraint. But is it realistic for an 8 kW monobloc? Typical 8 kW outdoor units weigh 60-100 kg, so maybe okay. But the module lists many components; does the total weight come to 90 kg? Not checked. But no explicit mismatch.\n\n9. Jurisdiction / compliance scope creep: They mention EU standards as mandatory, but the UK post-Brexit has its own UKCA marking. The report is for UK market, but still referencing PED 2014/68/EU (EU directive) as mandatory. In the UK, the Pressure Equipment (Safety) Regulations 2016 (UK SI 2016/1105) applies, which is essentially the same, but referencing EU directives might not be legally correct. However, it's common to reference both. The compliance gate warns about jurisdiction conflict: \"Brief targets multiple regions (UK, EU) without an explicit harmonisation plan.\" So that's a HIGH regulatory gap, but it's flagged. Might still be an error.\n\n10. Missing mandatory standard: The report says \"WARN · EMC 2014/30/EU (missing_mandatory): Electromagnetic Compatibility Directive (EU).\" For UK, the equivalent regulation is the Electromagnetic Compatibility Regulations 2016 (UK SI 2016/1091). So again, referencing EU directive may be incorrect for UK market. But if they are also selling into EU, they need both. The brief says \"UK market\", so reference to EU directives might be a jurisdiction error. The report lists EU 517/2014 (F-gas) as mandatory. In UK, F-gas regulation is the Fluorinated Greenhouse Gases Regulations 2015 (as amended) with UK quotas. So using EU regulation references for UK market is a jurisdiction error. This is a HIGH regulatory gap because it could lead to non-compliance. The compliance gate should have detected UK-specific standards. The report notes \"derived jurisdictions (UK, EU)\" but the design brief is for UK. It should use UK regulations. The compliance gate G1b uses class-standards.ts; maybe it doesn't differentiate between UK and EU enough. We can flag this.\n\n11. Refrigerant charge: 650g of R290. For A3 refrigerant, EN 378 limits charge based on room volume and system design. For an outdoor unit, charge is less restricted, but still limits exist. 650g is below the usual 5x LFL charge limit for indoor? Not sure, but 650g may be okay for an outdoor monobloc unit where the refrigerant circuit is outside. So not a big error.\n\n12. Component mismatch: In Module 4, the compressor is shown as \"Highly EHV-28CC-R290\" and \"Highly Compressor\" with £113.20 unit price. The appendix A recommends ASH286MV-C8DU or ATH280SD-C9EU. This is a duplicate and likely fake part number. That's internal coherence and procurement.\n\nNow, to format findings. Need to be specific, with section name, evidence, root cause in chain, fix recommendation.\n\nLet's list findings:\n\nFinding 1: (HIGH) Internal coherence — Duplicate compressor entries in BoM with identical part number but different costs.\n- Evidence: Module 4 BoM page 5 lists \"Bldc Compressor Motor\" £17.08 and \"Highly Compressor\" £113.20, both part number EHV-28CC-R290.\n- Root cause: Generator hallucinated a separate motor and compressor assembly when both are the same component; likely the research synthesis or generator stage conflated the compressor motor and the compressor body.\n- Fix: In stage 2 (modules generation) or the BoM aggregation step, enforce uniqueness of part numbers and flag duplicates; a post-BoM coherence check (G3 maybe) should catch repeated PNs with different costs.\n\nFinding 2: (HIGH) Procurement realism — Majority of part numbers are fabricated and unverifiable.\n- Evidence: Appendix B notes \"67 part numbers could not be verified\" and many stripped; BoM is populated with fake SKUs like EV-8K-R290, B5-24-R290, etc.\n- Root cause: The generator stage produces plausible-sounding SKUs without sufficient reference to real manufacturer catalogues; the part-verification engine (Stage 4.5) is too weak in stripping fakes and providing real replacements.\n- Fix: Integrate a stricter part-number verification using live distributor APIs (DigiKey, Mouser, Farnell) before BoM finalization; if a part cannot be verified, it should be flagged as TBD with a specification, not a fabricated number. Improve Engine D (supplier lookup) to require at least one confirmed source.\n\nFinding 3: (MEDIUM) Cost realism — Unrealistic unit prices with suspicious uniform values (e.g., £17.24, £7.81, £13.42).\n- Evidence: BoM shows many items priced at exactly £17.24, £7.81, £4.64, £0.21, etc., and only 12 actual distributor quotes out of 141 lines.\n- Root cause: Engine B (price estimates) likely falls back to default or poorly calibrated web estimates; the \"web judgement\" source is producing generic values not tied to actual market data.\n- Fix: Calibrate Engine B with actual distributor pricing data for common HVAC components, and apply a flag if the confidence is low; require at least 50% actual quotes or limit estimation to ±50% reference.\n\nFinding 4: (HIGH) Internal coherence — Cost benchmark uses 30 kW units for an 8 kW product, mismatched scale.\n- Evidence: On BoM page 1: \"Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) ... = £600-900/kW. ... 30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics. Multiplier 4.36× overshoots Grok market by >400%\". The report later claims design's £430/kW is 28% below typical £600-£900, but that typical is derived from 30 kW data, not 8 kW.\n- Root cause: The cost-stack benchmarking (likely Engine A cost-reality G2) incorrectly matched the product to the wrong class scale (30 kW class) in the reference corpus, leading to an invalid comparison.\n- Fix: Update Engine C reference anchor to enforce power-class matching (e.g., within ±20% of design thermal output) before generating benchmark commentary; modify class-floors.ts to include explicit kW bins for cost-per-kW references.\n\nFinding 5: (HIGH) UK MCS / regulatory gap — Mandatory UK-specific standards not applied; EU directives referenced for UK market.\n- Evidence: The Regulatory section lists EU 517/2014 (F-gas), PED 2014/68/EU, MD 2006/42/EC, etc., but the brief is only for UK. UK has separate regulations (e.g., Fluorinated Greenhouse Gases Regulations 2015, Pressure Equipment (Safety) Regulations 2016). The compliance gate warns \"EMC 2014/30/EU missing\" but doesn't suggest UK equivalent EMC Regulations 2016.\n- Root cause: The G1b compliance gate (class-standards.ts) is only keyed on \"EU\" jurisdiction, not on \"UK\" post-Brexit, applying EU standards when UK-specific ones should be used for CE/UKCA marking.\n- Fix: Update class-standards.ts to include a UK jurisdiction path that maps to UK statutory instruments (e.g., UK SI 2016/1105 for pressure equipment, UK SI 2016/1091 for EMC, etc.) and require UKCA marking where applicable. Brief parse should detect \"UK\" and trigger this path.\n\nFinding 6: (MEDIUM) Physics plausibility — Low defrost heater power (100 W) may be insufficient to prevent ice buildup in extreme conditions.\n- Evidence: Module 2.2 lists a Raychem QTVR-100W defrost heater cable of 100 W for the condensate drip pan. While the system uses reverse-cycle defrost, the drip pan may need heating to prevent ice accumulation. 100 W over ~0.45 m² pan may be borderline in -25°C.\n- Root cause: The brief-to-design derivation (G0 physics ledger or G0.5 reconciliation) might not have validated the drip pan heater requirement against climate boundary conditions; the design specification just accepts 100 W without engineering justification.\n- Fix: In the G0 physics ledger, add a thermal balance check for condensate management at minimum ambient temperature; require a minimum power density (e.g., 200 W/m²) based on icing conditions, and flag if design deviates significantly.\n\nFinding 7: (HIGH) BoM realism — Insufficient quantity of refrigerant cylinder; the BoM line item treats a 5 kg cylinder as a per-unit direct material, but one cylinder can charge 7-8 units (650 g each).\n- Evidence: Module 3 BoM, \"R290-IND-5KG\" qty 1, unit £17.24, line £17.24 for 650 g charge. At that price, it's as if buying one cylinder per unit. Realistic cost should be per charge (e.g., £17.24 / (5000/650) ≈ £2.24 per unit).\n- Root cause: The BoM generation (Engine B) did not amortize bulk consumables correctly; it treated the cylinder as a single-use part.\n- Fix: Add an amortization rule in Engine B for consumable lines (refrigerant, brazing rods, etc.) that computes per-unit cost based on container/unit usage ratio, with a default if usage not specified.\n\nFinding 8: (MEDIUM) Internal coherence — Missing cross-module connection specified in safety interlock but not accounted in BoM wiring.\n- Evidence: K10 shadow graph says missing required edge \"safety_protection ” [Digital-24V] energy_conversion_transduction — HP switch ... hard-wired to compressor contactor coil\" is missing. Both modules list components, but no explicit wire/harness linking them.\n- Root cause: The structural-gate router (G4 grammar gate) didn't enforce that a required edge in the K10 graph gets a physical part (wire/cable) in the BoM; likely out-of-scope or shadow mode only.\n- Fix: Promote K10 to enforcing mode and require that each required edge in the class graph has a corresponding wire/harness part in the BoM; the structural-gate router should add a generic \"control wiring harness\" if not explicit.\n\nThese are the main findings. I will present them in the required format, with severity, section, evidence, root cause, and fix recommendation.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We are asked to review an AI-generated engineering report for a residential air-source heat pump, identify errors/inconsistencies, and recommend code fixes. The report is quite long. We need to be brutal and focus on specific categories. Let's go through systematically.\n\nCategories: physics/thermo, UK MCS/regulatory, BoM realism, internal coherence, brief-vs-design mismatch, procurement realism, compliance scope/jurisdiction errors.\n\nI'll read the report, looking for issues. The report is for an 8 kW R290 ASHP for UK market.\n\nKey issues I see:\n\n1. Brief-vs-design mismatch: The brief says \"target rated thermal output 8 kW\" and the design parameters show 8 kW, but is that plausible for the components chosen? The compressor has \"2.5 kW electrical input\" (module 4, compressor and drives: Highly EHV-28CC-R290, rated 2.5 kW electrical input). With SCOP 3.5, thermal output would be 2.5 * 3.5 = 8.75 kW, but the SCOP is seasonal coefficient, not instantaneous COP. However, at design conditions, the COP should be higher than SCOP, so 2.5 kW might be plausible. But is the compressor rated 2.5 kW electrical or thermal? In compressor specs, often \"rated input power\" is electrical. So 2.5 kW electrical input at what condition? For an 8 kW heat pump, typical electrical input at rated conditions might be around 2.0-2.5 kW with COP of 3.5-4.0. So that seems okay. But check the compressor model: Highly EHV-28CC-R290. 28CC might indicate displacement 28 cc. For R290, typical compressor displacement for 8 kW might be larger. Let's see if that's plausible. I can't verify exact model, but it's a common series. Might be okay. But note that later in appendix A, the compressor is \"stripped\" and recommended as ATH280SD-C9EU or ASH286MV-C8DU. Those might have different ratings. I'll not flag that as HIGH unless obvious. But there is a potential mismatch: The compressor is listed twice? In module 4 sub-module \"Compressor and drives\", they list \"Highly bldc compressor motor (part EHV-28CC-R290)\" and then also \"Highly Compressor (part EHV-28CC-R290)\" in the BoM? Actually looking at BoM page 5: in Compressor and Drives, they have \"Bldc Compressor Motor\" line with Highly EHV-28CC-R290, £17.08, and \"Highly Compressor\" line with the same part number, £113.20. This is duplicate? Possibly the motor and the compressor shell are separate? But it says \"Highly Compressor\" as a separate line item, and the Bldc Compressor Motor might be the motor itself. But then the same part number appears twice. That could be an internal coherence error.\n\n2. Internal coherence: Module 4 lists a \"bldc compressor motor\" as a component of the compressor sub-module, and also a \"Highly compressor\" as a component. In the BoM, both appear as separate line items with different unit costs (£17.08 vs £113.20) but the same part number (EHV-28CC-R290). This is contradictory. Also, in appendix A, both are flagged as stripped, with different recommended replacements (ATH280SD-C9EU and ASH286MV-C8DU). So the AI generated two separate parts erroneously.\n\n3. BoM realism: Many unit prices are implausibly low or high. For example, the refrigerant charge: BOC R290-IND-5KG with 650g charge, unit price ~£17.24 for 5 kg cylinder, but they only charge 650g, so cost per charge is just £17.24? Likely they'd buy bulk or per cylinder. £17.24 for a 5 kg cylinder seems low; a full 5 kg R290 cylinder might be ~£100+. And they priced at £17.24 per unit? They might be using the cylinder cost as the line item, but they'd need one cylinder per unit? No, one cylinder can fill many heat pumps. The BoM price should reflect the cost of 650g of refrigerant, not the whole cylinder. If the cylinder costs £100, 650g would be £13. So maybe £17.24 is plausible. But they have \"R290-IND-5KG\" as the part, quantity 1, unit £17.24, line £17.24. That seems to treat the cylinder as a direct material cost. That's okay if they amortize the cylinder. Not a major error. However, many prices are suspiciously uniform: many web estimates are £17.24, £7.81, £13.42, £4.64, £0.21, £0.01. These look like placeholder values, not real prices. The BoM says \"129 web estimates\", but the unit prices are mostly ~17.24, 7.81, 13.42, etc. These are likely generated by the engine's price estimator without real data. That's a realism issue. The report notes \"Scale factor: 0.325 — per-class batch-economics multiplier applied to distributor-quoted lines\". But the BoM is full of estimates. The cost stack shows raw BoM £1,762.21, with OEM price £2,234.27, which seems very low for an 8 kW ASHP. A real 8 kW monobloc typically costs OEM around £2,500-£3,500? But they claim £430/kW installed, 28% below typical range £600-£900. That's for a 30 kW unit mis-scaled? Actually the small domestic 8 kW unit might be £600-900/kW installed, so £430/kW would be unrealistic. They claim it's within engineering noise but then flag \"Multiplier 4.36× overshoots Grok market by >400% — both the raw BoM band ... and the COST_STACK install factor (I=0.80) appear too generous for the 30 kW class.\" That's incoherent: they're comparing with 30 kW light-commercial units? The report later in the BoM summary says \"Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) + scaled pipework + electrics + MCS commissioning = £18-27k total = £600-900/kW. 30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics.\" So they derived the £600-900/kW from 30 kW units, not 8 kW. That's a scale mismatch. The installed ASP of £3,441 for 8 kW corresponds to £430/kW, but the typical range for 8 kW might be similar £600-900, so the design is 28% below that. But the justification uses 30 kW data, which is smaller £/kW? Actually 30 kW units would have lower £/kW due to economies, so £600-900 for 30 kW means for 8 kW it would be even higher, so the design's £430/kW is even more unrealistic. So this is an internal coherence error (using wrong comparison scale) and a cost realism issue. But the main error: the installed cost seems too low.\n\n4. UK MCS regulatory gap: The compliance section lists BS EN 14825, BS EN 14511, etc., but the report says \"UK MCS\" is not yet added as mandatory in the class registry, but it's \"de-facto mandatory for residential UK market.\" However, they note a WARN: \"1 mandatory standard not declared in the brief for the detected jurisdictions (UK, EU).\" That is EMC 2014/30/EU. So that's a gap; they need EMC directive compliance. That's a HIGH regulatory gap. The report itself notes it, but it's still missing from the design. The product must comply. The BoM includes EMC filters and ferrite chokes, so maybe they're addressing it, but the directive is not formally included in the brief. This could be a HIGH regulatory gap, but it's flagged. Since the review is to find errors, we can note it.\n\n5. Physics/thermo: Any violations? The defrost heater is 100 W. Is that enough to defrost an 8 kW unit? Typically defrost energy may be a few kW for a short time, but 100 W seems low. However, a 100 W trace heater on the drip pan may be separate from a reverse-cycle defrost that uses hot gas. The report says the defrost cycle uses the 4-way reversing valve for active defrost (Module 3). So the drip pan heater is just to prevent ice buildup in the pan. 100 W could be plausible for that. But the Raychem QTVR-100W is a 100 W heating cable, 4 m length. That might be fine. No obvious physics error.\n\n6. Internal coherence: Multiple references to \"30 kW\" in the cost justification while the design is 8 kW. The report on page 1 of BoM: \"Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: ...\" then \"30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics. Multiplier 4.36× overshoots Grok market by >400% ... Flagged for COST_STACK calibration.\" This is nonsense mixing scales. So internal coherence error between 8 kW and 30 kW.\n\n7. Procurement realism: Many part numbers are fabricated. The report itself says 67 stripped and many uncertain. For a \"startup paying £20/month for this PDF\", the BoM is full of fabricated SKUs, which is a high procurement realism error. We should call out that the engine is generating fake part numbers. The fix is in the part-verification engine (Stage 4.5 maybe) and the supplier lookup (Engine D). The chain should not output a BoM with fabricated numbers; it should flag TBD or not generate them. This is a major failing.\n\n8. Brief-vs-design mismatch: The brief specifies a maximum gross mass of 90 kg. The report says that's the constraint. But is it realistic for an 8 kW monobloc? Typical 8 kW outdoor units weigh 60-100 kg, so maybe okay. But the module lists many components; does the total weight come to 90 kg? Not checked. But no explicit mismatch.\n\n9. Jurisdiction / compliance scope creep: They mention EU standards as mandatory, but the UK post-Brexit has its own UKCA marking. The report is for UK market, but still referencing PED 2014/68/EU (EU directive) as mandatory. In the UK, the Pressure Equipment (Safety) Regulations 2016 (UK SI 2016/1105) applies, which is essentially the same, but referencing EU directives might not be legally correct. However, it's common to reference both. The compliance gate warns about jurisdiction conflict: \"Brief targets multiple regions (UK, EU) without an explicit harmonisation plan.\" So that's a HIGH regulatory gap, but it's flagged. Might still be an error.\n\n10. Missing mandatory standard: The report says \"WARN · EMC 2014/30/EU (missing_mandatory): Electromagnetic Compatibility Directive (EU).\" For UK, the equivalent regulation is the Electromagnetic Compatibility Regulations 2016 (UK SI 2016/1091). So again, referencing EU directive may be incorrect for UK market. But if they are also selling into EU, they need both. The brief says \"UK market\", so reference to EU directives might be a jurisdiction error. The report lists EU 517/2014 (F-gas) as mandatory. In UK, F-gas regulation is the Fluorinated Greenhouse Gases Regulations 2015 (as amended) with UK quotas. So using EU regulation references for UK market is a jurisdiction error. This is a HIGH regulatory gap because it could lead to non-compliance. The compliance gate should have detected UK-specific standards. The report notes \"derived jurisdictions (UK, EU)\" but the design brief is for UK. It should use UK regulations. The compliance gate G1b uses class-standards.ts; maybe it doesn't differentiate between UK and EU enough. We can flag this.\n\n11. Refrigerant charge: 650g of R290. For A3 refrigerant, EN 378 limits charge based on room volume and system design. For an outdoor unit, charge is less restricted, but still limits exist. 650g is below the usual 5x LFL charge limit for indoor? Not sure, but 650g may be okay for an outdoor monobloc unit where the refrigerant circuit is outside. So not a big error.\n\n12. Component mismatch: In Module 4, the compressor is shown as \"Highly EHV-28CC-R290\" and \"Highly Compressor\" with £113.20 unit price. The appendix A recommends ASH286MV-C8DU or ATH280SD-C9EU. This is a duplicate and likely fake part number. That's internal coherence and procurement.\n\nNow, to format findings. Need to be specific, with section name, evidence, root cause in chain, fix recommendation.\n\nLet's list findings:\n\nFinding 1: (HIGH) Internal coherence — Duplicate compressor entries in BoM with identical part number but different costs.\n- Evidence: Module 4 BoM page 5 lists \"Bldc Compressor Motor\" £17.08 and \"Highly Compressor\" £113.20, both part number EHV-28CC-R290.\n- Root cause: Generator hallucinated a separate motor and compressor assembly when both are the same component; likely the research synthesis or generator stage conflated the compressor motor and the compressor body.\n- Fix: In stage 2 (modules generation) or the BoM aggregation step, enforce uniqueness of part numbers and flag duplicates; a post-BoM coherence check (G3 maybe) should catch repeated PNs with different costs.\n\nFinding 2: (HIGH) Procurement realism — Majority of part numbers are fabricated and unverifiable.\n- Evidence: Appendix B notes \"67 part numbers could not be verified\" and many stripped; BoM is populated with fake SKUs like EV-8K-R290, B5-24-R290, etc.\n- Root cause: The generator stage produces plausible-sounding SKUs without sufficient reference to real manufacturer catalogues; the part-verification engine (Stage 4.5) is too weak in stripping fakes and providing real replacements.\n- Fix: Integrate a stricter part-number verification using live distributor APIs (DigiKey, Mouser, Farnell) before BoM finalization; if a part cannot be verified, it should be flagged as TBD with a specification, not a fabricated number. Improve Engine D (supplier lookup) to require at least one confirmed source.\n\nFinding 3: (MEDIUM) Cost realism — Unrealistic unit prices with suspicious uniform values (e.g., £17.24, £7.81, £13.42).\n- Evidence: BoM shows many items priced at exactly £17.24, £7.81, £4.64, £0.21, etc., and only 12 actual distributor quotes out of 141 lines.\n- Root cause: Engine B (price estimates) likely falls back to default or poorly calibrated web estimates; the \"web judgement\" source is producing generic values not tied to actual market data.\n- Fix: Calibrate Engine B with actual distributor pricing data for common HVAC components, and apply a flag if the confidence is low; require at least 50% actual quotes or limit estimation to ±50% reference.\n\nFinding 4: (HIGH) Internal coherence — Cost benchmark uses 30 kW units for an 8 kW product, mismatched scale.\n- Evidence: On BoM page 1: \"Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) ... = £600-900/kW. ... 30 kW units show clear economies vs small <10 kW domestics. Multiplier 4.36× overshoots Grok market by >400%\". The report later claims design's £430/kW is 28% below typical £600-£900, but that typical is derived from 30 kW data, not 8 kW.\n- Root cause: The cost-stack benchmarking (likely Engine A cost-reality G2) incorrectly matched the product to the wrong class scale (30 kW class) in the reference corpus, leading to an invalid comparison.\n- Fix: Update Engine C reference anchor to enforce power-class matching (e.g., within ±20% of design thermal output) before generating benchmark commentary; modify class-floors.ts to include explicit kW bins for cost-per-kW references.\n\nFinding 5: (HIGH) UK MCS / regulatory gap — Mandatory UK-specific standards not applied; EU directives referenced for UK market.\n- Evidence: The Regulatory section lists EU 517/2014 (F-gas), PED 2014/68/EU, MD 2006/42/EC, etc., but the brief is only for UK. UK has separate regulations (e.g., Fluorinated Greenhouse Gases Regulations 2015, Pressure Equipment (Safety) Regulations 2016). The compliance gate warns \"EMC 2014/30/EU missing\" but doesn't suggest UK equivalent EMC Regulations 2016.\n- Root cause: The G1b compliance gate (class-standards.ts) is only keyed on \"EU\" jurisdiction, not on \"UK\" post-Brexit, applying EU standards when UK-specific ones should be used for CE/UKCA marking.\n- Fix: Update class-standards.ts to include a UK jurisdiction path that maps to UK statutory instruments (e.g., UK SI 2016/1105 for pressure equipment, UK SI 2016/1091 for EMC, etc.) and require UKCA marking where applicable. Brief parse should detect \"UK\" and trigger this path.\n\nFinding 6: (MEDIUM) Physics plausibility — Low defrost heater power (100 W) may be insufficient to prevent ice buildup in extreme conditions.\n- Evidence: Module 2.2 lists a Raychem QTVR-100W defrost heater cable of 100 W for the condensate drip pan. While the system uses reverse-cycle defrost, the drip pan may need heating to prevent ice accumulation. 100 W over ~0.45 m² pan may be borderline in -25°C.\n- Root cause: The brief-to-design derivation (G0 physics ledger or G0.5 reconciliation) might not have validated the drip pan heater requirement against climate boundary conditions; the design specification just accepts 100 W without engineering justification.\n- Fix: In the G0 physics ledger, add a thermal balance check for condensate management at minimum ambient temperature; require a minimum power density (e.g., 200 W/m²) based on icing conditions, and flag if design deviates significantly.\n\nFinding 7: (HIGH) BoM realism — Insufficient quantity of refrigerant cylinder; the BoM line item treats a 5 kg cylinder as a per-unit direct material, but one cylinder can charge 7-8 units (650 g each).\n- Evidence: Module 3 BoM, \"R290-IND-5KG\" qty 1, unit £17.24, line £17.24 for 650 g charge. At that price, it's as if buying one cylinder per unit. Realistic cost should be per charge (e.g., £17.24 / (5000/650) ≈ £2.24 per unit).\n- Root cause: The BoM generation (Engine B) did not amortize bulk consumables correctly; it treated the cylinder as a single-use part.\n- Fix: Add an amortization rule in Engine B for consumable lines (refrigerant, brazing rods, etc.) that computes per-unit cost based on container/unit usage ratio, with a default if usage not specified.\n\nFinding 8: (MEDIUM) Internal coherence — Missing cross-module connection specified in safety interlock but not accounted in BoM wiring.\n- Evidence: K10 shadow graph says missing required edge \"safety_protection ” [Digital-24V] energy_conversion_transduction — HP switch ... hard-wired to compressor contactor coil\" is missing. Both modules list components, but no explicit wire/harness linking them.\n- Root cause: The structural-gate router (G4 grammar gate) didn't enforce that a required edge in the K10 graph gets a physical part (wire/cable) in the BoM; likely out-of-scope or shadow mode only.\n- Fix: Promote K10 to enforcing mode and require that each required edge in the class graph has a corresponding wire/harness part in the BoM; the structural-gate router should add a generic \"control wiring harness\" if not explicit.\n\nThese are the main findings. I will present them in the required format, with severity, section, evidence, root cause, and fix recommendation.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":36225,"completion_tokens":6378,"total_tokens":42603,"cost":0.021306735,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.021306735,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.015757875,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.00554886},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4490,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}